// ============================================================================
//  Desktop control policy  --  read fresh on EVERY desktop_* tool call.
//  Edit this file and save: the change applies to the very next tool call.
//  No restart needed. This is your live leash.
//
//  Emergency stop:  set "enabled": false  (or just delete/rename this file --
//                   a missing or malformed policy denies everything).
//
//  HOW A DECISION IS MADE
//  Three dimensions are evaluated independently, then the MOST RESTRICTIVE
//  result wins:
//        1. capabilities  -- may the agent do this kind of thing at all?
//        2. workspaces    -- is the target on a workspace it may touch?
//        3. apps          -- may it do this to this particular window?
//
//  Within one dimension, the LAST matching pattern wins. So put broad rules
//  first and exceptions last.
//
//  Actions:  "allow"  do it silently
//            "ask"    pop the approval overlay on the bar before doing it
//            "deny"   refuse, and tell the agent why
//
//  Anything not matched by any pattern is DENIED. That is deliberate.
//  Every refusal prints the exact rule that caused it, so run
//  `desktop_policy` if you are unsure why something was blocked.
//
//  WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT DO
//  It governs what the TOOLS do. It has no channel into the agent's own
//  instructions, and an agent can refuse something this file allows.
//
//  That distinction has cost real time, so it is worth stating plainly:
//  someone spent four rounds editing capabilities here trying to let an agent
//  log into a dev app, while the actual rule sat in an agent definition
//  (~/.claude/agents/*.md for Claude Code, and the equivalent for other
//  runners). This file looked like the permission system because it is the one
//  with capability names in it.
//
//  So if an agent refuses and no rule below explains it, the refusal is coming
//  from its instructions, not from here. `desktop_policy` prints what this
//  file decided; anything it does not account for is somewhere else.
//
//  For credentials specifically there is now a path that needs no rule
//  relaxed anywhere: store one with `desktop-agent-config secret-add <name>`
//  and the agent enters it by NAME, never learning the value. See "secret"
//  under capabilities.
// ============================================================================
{
  // Master switch. false => every desktop_* tool refuses immediately.
  //
  // On by default, and the safety does not come from this line: every gated
  // action still stops and asks you, denied commands still refuse, and the
  // full-access lease below is off. Shipping this false made a fresh install
  // refuse everything while the panel reported itself as armed -- a default
  // whose only effect was to make the plugin look broken. Toggle it from the
  // panel's Policy tab, or from the bar icon in an emergency.
  "enabled": true,

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  YOLO -- a time-boxed grant that stops the agent interrupting you.
  //
  //  Granted from the bar: click the Desktop Agent icon and pick a duration.
  //  While the lease runs, every "ask" below is auto-approved silently.
  //
  //  What a lease does NOT do, ever:
  //    * it does not touch "deny". Password managers, 2FA windows, typing into
  //      terminals, special workspaces and Claude Code's own window stay
  //      refused exactly as they are now. The lease removes the interruption,
  //      never the boundary.
  //    * it does not auto-approve destructive commands. rm, dd, mkfs, chmod,
  //      kill, systemctl, shutdown, package managers, curl/wget and the rest
  //      still raise the overlay while yolo is on -- so the irreversible step
  //      stays the one thing you are shown. That list lives in server.ts as
  //      NEVER_YOLO and is not editable from here on purpose. If you actually
  //      want one of them unattended, name it here: "rm": "allow" in
  //      run.commands works, because then you chose it rather than a timer.
  //
  //  The lease is a timestamp in ~/.local/state/desktop-agent/yolo.json. It
  //  expires by itself with nothing running -- deleting that file, or flipping
  //  the kill switch, ends it immediately.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "yolo": {
    // Set false and no lease can ever take effect, whatever the bar says.
    "enabled": false,
    // Hard ceiling on a single lease, re-checked on every call. A lease longer
    // than this is clamped, not honoured.
    "maxMinutes": 60
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  CAPABILITIES -- what kinds of action are permitted, ignoring the target.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "capabilities": {
    "observe": "allow", // list windows, monitors, workspaces, cursor position
    "screenshot": "allow", // capture the screen (denied apps are blacked out)
    "workspace": "allow", // switch/rename workspaces
    "focus": "allow", // move focus to a window
    // Anything that changes your session asks the first time, every time.
    //
    // A long session of this is genuinely tedious -- that is what the timed
    // full-access lease is for: grant it, work, let it expire. The wrong fix
    // is to leave these on "allow" permanently, which is what an earlier demo
    // relaxation did: it shipped here for a while, so a fresh install handed
    // the agent silent mouse, keyboard and window control.
    //
    // Change them from the panel's Policy tab, not by hand.
    "manage": "ask", // move, resize, float, fullscreen, close, kill
    "launch": "ask", // start a new application
    "type": "ask", // enter text
    "key": "ask", // press keys and shortcuts
    "mouse": "ask", // move the cursor, click, scroll, drag
    // Both are gated a second time below -- "run" per command, "write" per
    // path -- and the deny lists there are the real protection, so a prompt on
    // every one of these would be noise on top of a check that already ran.
    "run": "allow", // run a command -- gated per-command in "run" below
    "write": "allow", // create/edit files -- gated per-path in "paths" below
    // The agent's OWN browser -- a separate empty profile, no extensions, none
    // of your logins. Reading pages through it replaces screenshots for
    // anything on the web. Starts at "ask" so you see the first few; there is
    // little reason to keep it there once you have watched it work.
    "browser": "ask",
    // Typing a password, token, PIN or recovery code.
    //
    // Separate from "type" because that one has to be usable: a setup where
    // every keystroke raises a card gets set to "allow" within a day, and then
    // a credential goes in with nothing shown. This asks whichever way "type"
    // is set, so you see the card once.
    //
    // A FULL-ACCESS LEASE COVERS IT. You granted that by hand, at the
    // keyboard, for the next hour, to get a piece of work done -- and being
    // stopped at a login box is precisely what you were buying your way past.
    // An earlier version held the card up anyway; that made the switch not
    // mean what it says, and made "review this app" impossible.
    //
    // A SCHEDULED JOB IS STILL REFUSED. That is the case worth refusing for:
    // nobody is awake to see it, so there is no seeing it. Grant it in the
    // moment, not on a timer.
    //
    // In the browser it is the field that decides, read over CDP: input
    // type=password, or an autocomplete of current-password, new-password or
    // one-time-code. On a native window there is nothing to read, so it
    // depends on the agent saying so.
    //
    // "allow" skips even the first card. Yours to set; nothing sets it for you.
    "secret": "ask"
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  AGENTS -- which caller identity may use which capability.
  //
  //  CAVEAT, read this before relying on it: MCP gives the server no way to
  //  tell which Claude Code subagent is calling. Every call therefore arrives
  //  under one identity, taken from $DESKTOP_AGENT_IDENTITY and defaulting to
  //  "agent". Run `desktop_policy` to see the identity in force.
  //
  //  The default used to be "claude" and changed when a second agent CLI could
  //  be selected. If you wrote rules under a "claude" key BEFORE that change,
  //  check them: an MCP entry whose env does not set DESKTOP_AGENT_IDENTITY
  //  now arrives as "agent" and will not match them. `desktop-agent
  //  mcp-install` writes the identity explicitly; entries installed earlier
  //  do not have it.
  //
  //  So this section is only useful if you run a second MCP entry with a
  //  different DESKTOP_AGENT_IDENTITY in its env. For a single entry it is
  //  effectively a global switch. Capability, workspace and app rules are the
  //  dimensions that actually do the work.
  //
  //  Leave it empty (or delete it) and every identity gets identical access.
  //  The moment you put one entry in it, it becomes an allowlist and any
  //  identity not matched is denied. Keys are globs matched against the
  //  identity; "*" inside a value sets every capability at once.
  //
  //      "claude":  { "*": "allow" },
  //      "ci":      { "observe": "allow", "screenshot": "allow", "*": "deny" }
  //
  //  IF YOU USE DELEGATION, match the subagents too. They arrive under their
  //  own identities -- "sub-1", "sub-2" and so on -- so an allowlist naming
  //  only your master locks every subagent out of everything, and a delegated
  //  batch fails in a way that looks like the feature is broken:
  //
  //      "claude":  { "*": "allow" },
  //      "sub-*":   { "observe": "allow", "screenshot": "allow", "run": "ask" }
  //
  //  Giving them less than the master is the point. They do headless work.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "agents": {},

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  WORKSPACES -- which workspaces the agent may observe and act on.
  //  Match on the name ("3", "special:magic") or "id:3".
  //
  //  To pen the agent into workspaces 8 and 9 only, replace the block with:
  //      "8": "allow",
  //      "9": "allow"
  //  ...and nothing else. Everything unmatched is denied.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "workspaces": {
    "*": "allow",
    "special:*": "deny" // scratchpads stay private
  },


  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  PATHS -- where desktop_write and desktop_edit may go.
  //
  //  Matched against the fully resolved absolute path: "~" is expanded, "." and
  //  ".." are collapsed, and symlinks are followed BEFORE matching -- so a link
  //  in an allowed directory cannot be used to reach a denied one.
  //
  //  Last match wins, and anything unmatched is denied. "*" spans "/" here, so
  //  "~/Work/*" already covers every depth beneath it.
  //
  //  SCOPE, so this is not mistaken for a sandbox: these rules govern the
  //  agent's own desktop_write and desktop_edit calls. They do NOT constrain a
  //  program the agent runs -- ffmpeg, git and tar write wherever they like.
  //  "run.commands" is the dimension that gates those.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "paths": {
    // Nothing by default. Say yes on purpose.
    "*": "ask",

    // Work the agent actually does.
    "~/Work/*": "allow",
    "~/Videos/*": "allow",
    "~/Pictures/*": "allow",
    "~/Documents/*": "allow",
    "~/Downloads/*": "allow",
    "~/Desktop/*": "allow",
    "/tmp/*": "allow",

    // The leash does not edit itself. Its own policy, its own system prompt,
    // its own lease -- an agent that can rewrite these is not restricted by
    // them, it is restricted until it decides otherwise.
    "~/.config/desktop-agent/*": "deny",
    "~/.claude/*": "deny",
    "~/.local/state/desktop-agent/*": "deny",
    "~/.local/bin/*": "deny", // desktop-yolo lives here

    // Secrets. The agent can already read most of these (cat is allowed); what
    // it must not do is plant a key or rewrite a known_hosts.
    "~/.ssh/*": "deny",
    "~/.gnupg/*": "deny",
    "~/.aws/*": "deny",
    "~/.netrc": "deny",
    "~/.git-credentials": "deny",
    "~/.npmrc": "deny",
    "*.env": "deny",
    "*/.env.*": "deny",
    "*credentials*": "deny",
    "*firebase-adminsdk*": "deny",

    // Anything that executes on its own the next time you log in or open a
    // shell. A write here is delayed code execution, not a file change.
    "~/.bashrc": "deny",
    "~/.zshrc": "deny",
    "~/.profile": "deny",
    "~/.bash_profile": "deny",
    "~/.zprofile": "deny",
    "~/.config/autostart/*": "deny",
    "~/.config/systemd/*": "deny",
    "~/.config/environment.d/*": "deny",
    "*/.git/hooks/*": "deny",

    // Break these and the desktop does not come back up.
    "~/.config/hypr/*": "deny",
    "~/.config/omarchy/*": "deny",
    "~/.config/uwsm/*": "deny",

    // Not the user's to edit, and root-owned anyway.
    "/etc/*": "deny",
    "/usr/*": "deny",
    "/boot/*": "deny",
    "/bin/*": "deny",
    "/sbin/*": "deny",
    "/lib/*": "deny",
    "/var/*": "deny",
    "/opt/*": "deny",
    "/sys/*": "deny",
    "/proc/*": "deny",
    "/dev/*": "deny",
    "/run/*": "deny"
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  WRITE -- limits that apply to every desktop_write and desktop_edit.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "write": {
    // Refuse a single write larger than this. Catches a runaway generation
    // before it fills the disk, not a determined agent.
    "maxBytes": 1000000,
    // Copy a file aside before overwriting or editing it. Backups go to
    // ~/.local/state/desktop-agent/backups/ so they do not litter your tree.
    "backup": true
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  APPS -- which windows the agent may see and control.
  //
  //  Patterns match against, in order of precision:
  //      "class:<class>"          e.g. "class:chromium"
  //      "initialclass:<class>"
  //      "title:<title>"          e.g. "title:*Gmail*"
  //      "initialtitle:<title>"
  //      "pid:<pid>"
  //  A pattern with no prefix is matched against the class AND the title.
  //
  //  A value is either a single action applied to all four verbs, or an object
  //  naming them individually:
  //      "see"     appear in window lists; visible in screenshots
  //      "focus"   may be focused
  //      "manage"  may be moved, resized, closed, killed
  //      "input"   may receive typing, key presses and clicks
  //
  //  Omitted verbs in an object fall back to "deny".
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "apps": {
    // Baseline: everything is visible and fully controllable...
    "*": "allow",

    // ...then carve out the things an agent must never touch.

    // Password managers and secret stores: fully invisible.
    "class:1Password": "deny",
    "class:*keepass*": "deny",
    "class:*bitwarden*": "deny",
    "class:org.gnome.Seahorse*": "deny",
    "title:*1Password*": "deny",
    "title:*Bitwarden*": "deny",

    // Banking and auth pages: invisible even though the browser is allowed.
    "title:*Sign in*": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow" },
    "title:*Login*": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow" },
    // ...but the Daltech staging apps are the thing being demonstrated, and both
    // carry DALTECH in the window title. This is narrower than relaxing the two
    // rules above, which would hand the agent every login page you ever open,
    // your bank included. It sits BEFORE the 2FA denies on purpose: last match
    // wins, so a Daltech window showing a one-time code is still refused.
    "title:*Daltech*": "allow",
    "title:*DALTECH*": "allow",

    "title:*2FA*": "deny",
    "title:*One-Time*": "deny",
    "title:*Authenticator*": "deny",

    // Terminals: readable and focusable, but never typed into. A shell is a
    // confused-deputy machine -- if the agent wants a command it has `bash`,
    // where you can see and gate it, instead of blind keystrokes.
    "class:org.wezfurlong.wezterm": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow" },
    "class:foot": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow" },
    "class:Alacritty": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow" },
    "class:com.mitchellh.ghostty": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow" },

    // The agent's OWN browser (see the "browser" section below). Its own empty
    // profile, none of your logins -- so unlike your Chromium above, it gets
    // full control of it. This is what makes a dedicated browser worth having:
    // the agent stops needing access to yours.
    "class:agent-browser": "allow",

    // Communication: let it read and click, but never send on your behalf
    // without a prompt. Change "input" to "allow" when you want it to reply.
    "class:chrome-app.slack.com*": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow", "input": "ask" },
    "class:chrome-mail.google.com*": { "see": "allow", "focus": "allow", "manage": "allow", "input": "ask" }
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  LAUNCH -- the only applications the agent may start, by friendly name.
  //  The agent passes a name, never a command line, so it cannot invent one.
  //  Add "args": true to let it append arguments (e.g. a URL to open).
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "launch": {
    "browser": { "command": ["chromium"], "args": true, "action": "ask" },
    "terminal": { "command": ["foot"], "action": "ask" },
    // args: true so it can open straight at a path it already located, rather
    // than launching at the default folder and hunting through the UI.
    "files": { "command": ["nautilus"], "args": true, "action": "ask" },
    "editor": { "command": ["nvim"], "action": "deny" }
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  FORBIDDEN KEYS -- refused even when "key" is "allow". Compared against
  //  the normalised chord, e.g. "SUPER SHIFT+e", so order of mods is fixed:
  //  SUPER CTRL ALT SHIFT.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "forbidKeys": [
    "SUPER SHIFT+e", // exit Hyprland
    "SUPER SHIFT+q", // kill session
    "CTRL ALT+Delete",
    "CTRL ALT+F*", // VT switch
    "SUPER+l", // lock screen
    "*+Print" // screenshot bindings
  ],

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  RUN -- which programs desktop_run may execute.
  //
  //  desktop_run takes a program plus an argument LIST, never a shell line.
  //  There is no pipe, no glob, no $(...) and no redirection, which is what
  //  makes the rules below mean what they say: the first token is always the
  //  real binary, so "bash" cannot be smuggled in as "sh -c ...".
  //
  //  Matching is the usual last-wins glob, against both the name as given and
  //  its basename. Anything unmatched is "ask", so a program nobody thought to
  //  list stops and prompts rather than silently running or silently failing.
  //
  //  Prefer this over driving a GUI: one `fd` beats a file-manager launch plus
  //  five screenshots and a guessed click.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "run": {
    // Kill a runaway command, and keep a huge output from flooding the model.
    "timeoutMs": 15000,
    "maxOutputBytes": 100000,

    "commands": {
      // Unlisted => ask. Fail-closed, same as every other dimension here.
      "*": "ask",

      // Reading and searching the filesystem: the whole point of this section.
      "ls": "allow",
      "eza": "allow",
      "find": "allow",
      "fd": "allow",
      "rg": "allow",
      "grep": "allow",
      "cat": "allow",
      "bat": "allow",
      "head": "allow",
      "tail": "allow",
      "wc": "allow",
      "stat": "allow",
      "file": "allow",
      "du": "allow",
      "df": "allow",
      "realpath": "allow",
      "basename": "allow",
      "dirname": "allow",
      "readlink": "allow",
      "which": "allow",
      "echo": "allow",
      "date": "allow",
      "jq": "allow",
      "git": "allow",
      "xdg-mime": "allow",

      // Inspect media without being able to write any. ffmpeg itself stays at
      // "ask" (it takes -y and an output path); ffprobe only ever reads.
      "ffprobe": "allow",

      // The bare "omarchy" command is a router over ~427 subcommands, and the
      // match here is on the binary name only -- the arguments are never read.
      // So "omarchy": "allow" would quietly re-open half the deny list below:
      //     omarchy hyprland ...      = hyprctl, denied by name
      //     omarchy shell <t> <m>     = qs ipc call, denied by name
      //     omarchy system shutdown   = shutdown, denied by name
      //     omarchy system factory reset
      //     omarchy pkg / install / remove / refresh
      // Kept at "ask" so the subcommand is on the overlay where you can read it,
      // and listed in NEVER_YOLO so a lease cannot promote it.
      "omarchy": "ask",

      // Same reasoning for the individual binaries, which are all on PATH at
      // /usr/share/omarchy/bin. Deny the family, then name the exceptions --
      // last match wins, so the allows below still stand.
      "omarchy-*": "deny",

      // Start and stop the screen recorder without an overlay in every clip.
      // omarchy-capture-screenrecording writes to ~/Videos and takes no shell.
      "omarchy-capture-screenrecording": "allow",

      // Theming is cosmetic, reversible, and the agent gets asked for it a lot.
      "omarchy-theme-list": "allow",
      "omarchy-theme-set": "allow",

      // ~/.local/bin/daltech-rec start|stop — a fixed two-branch wrapper.
      //
      // Needed because gpu-screen-recorder inherits the caller's stdout (only its
      // stderr is redirected), so desktop_run waits for an EOF that never arrives
      // and times out at 15s while the recording is in fact running. desktop_run
      // passes a program and an argument list, never a shell line, so it cannot
      // redirect — the wrapper is the only place that can.
      //
      // It takes no free-form input: anything other than start|stop exits 2.
      "daltech-rec": "allow",

      // ----------------------------------------------------------------------
      // Denied because they are an UNGATED COPY of the desktop tools. Letting
      // these through would mean two doors to the same room, one of them with
      // no approval prompt, no audit line and no screenshot redaction.
      "hyprctl": "deny", // = desktop_window / desktop_workspace / desktop_mouse
      "ydotool": "deny", // = desktop_mouse / desktop_key
      "wtype": "deny", // = desktop_type
      "dotool": "deny",
      "wlrctl": "deny",
      // = desktop_screenshot, but with NO redaction. If you want a PNG on disk
      // to look at afterwards, that is desktop_screenshot with save: true --
      // it keeps the REDACTED capture. Allowing grim to get artifacts trades
      // away the password managers and 2FA windows the redaction exists for.
      "grim": "deny",
      "slurp": "deny",
      "wf-recorder": "deny",
      "wl-copy": "deny", // clipboard is how desktop_type moves text
      "wl-paste": "deny",
      "qs": "deny", // would drive the approval overlay itself
      "quickshell": "deny",
      "desktop-yolo": "deny", // would let the agent grant itself a free pass
      // The panel's own controls, by another name. These rewrite this file --
      // the policy, the capability table, the lease switch -- so an agent that
      // can run them is not governed by any of it. desktop_run refuses them in
      // code as well, because a rule living in the file they rewrite is not a
      // boundary; these are here so desktop_policy can explain the refusal.
      "desktop-agent": "deny",
      "desktop-agent-config": "deny",
      "desktop-agent-arm": "deny",
      // (omarchy-* is already denied as a family above; these stay for the
      // record of why -- they are desktop_launch without the gate.)
      "omarchy-launch-editor": "deny",
      "omarchy-launch-tui": "deny",
      "uwsm-app": "deny",
      "setsid": "deny",
      "nohup": "deny",

      // Denied because they re-enter a shell and launder every rule above.
      // This is the line that makes the denials above hold.
      "sh": "deny",
      "bash": "deny",
      "zsh": "deny",
      "fish": "deny",
      "dash": "deny",
      "env": "deny",
      "xargs": "deny",
      "find -exec": "deny", // cosmetic reminder; find itself is allowed
      "perl": "deny",
      "python": "deny",
      "python3": "deny",
      "ruby": "deny",
      "node": "deny",
      "bun": "deny",
      "awk": "deny", // awk system() is a shell
      "gawk": "deny",
      "ssh": "deny",

      // Denied because they escalate.
      "sudo": "deny",
      "pkexec": "deny",
      "doas": "deny",
      "su": "deny"
    }
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  BROWSER -- the agent's own browser, driven over the DevTools protocol.
  //
  //  It is a SEPARATE browser from yours: its own profile under
  //  ~/.local/state/desktop-agent/browser/, no extensions, no cookies, no
  //  saved passwords, none of your history. The agent can move around in it
  //  freely because there is nothing of yours inside it to reach.
  //
  //  Two things this deliberately does NOT do:
  //
  //    * It never attaches to a browser it did not start. Chromium sits under
  //      1Password, Obsidian, your IDE and every other Electron app on this
  //      machine, so a tool that attached to "any debugging port" would read
  //      straight through the "apps" rules above that exist to keep the agent
  //      out of exactly those windows. One endpoint, its own, or none.
  //
  //    * It does not make actions safe, only reads. An empty profile contains
  //      nothing to leak, but the network is still the real network: a form
  //      submitted from this browser is submitted for real. If you log it into
  //      an account, that account's session lives in the profile from then on
  //      -- so decide deliberately what, if anything, it may sign into.
  //
  //  The window carries class "agent-browser", so the "apps" section above can
  //  treat it differently from your own Chromium. That is why this entry is in
  //  "apps": full control of its own browser, none of yours.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "browser": {
    // Which browser to run. Empty = first of chromium/chrome/brave on PATH.
    // Must be Chromium-family; Firefox does not speak this protocol.
    "command": "",
    // true hides the window entirely. Handy for background work, but you then
    // cannot see what the agent is doing, so it defaults to visible.
    "headless": false
  },

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  SAFETY RAILS
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------

  // Never let the agent see or touch the terminal window Claude Code itself
  // is running in. Resolved live by walking this server's process ancestry,
  // so it keeps working if you move or retitle the window.
  "protectSelf": true,

  // Refuse to enter more than this many characters in one call.
  "maxTextLength": 4000,

  // Screenshot handling.
  "screenshot": {
    // Downscale wider captures before sending to the model. Keeps cost sane;
    // 1920 keeps on-screen text legible. Set 0 to send at native resolution.
    "maxWidth": 1920,
    // Black out any window the agent may not "see", instead of refusing the
    // whole capture. Set false to refuse instead.
    "redactDenied": true
  },

  // Write one line per allowed action to ~/.local/share/desktop-agent/desktop.log
  // so there is an audit trail of everything the agent did to your desktop.
  "audit": true
}
